\begin{note}
When python evaluates \lstinline{a + b},
it first tries \lstinline{a.__add__(b)}.
If that returns \lstinline{NotImplemented}
(or if \lstinline{a} does not define \lstinline{__add__}),
python tries the \emph{reflected} operation \lstinline{b.__radd__(a)}.
This is what allows \lstinline{2 * Vec(1, 2)} to work
even though \lstinline{int} does not know how to multiply itself by a \lstinline{Vec}.
\end{note}
